10:32:59 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: Shoes and vehicle tires are both rubber-on-road products, but tires are designed and built for far longer service. Key reasons: - **Tires have much more tread to sacrifice.** A new car tire may have roughly 8–12 mm of tread depth, over a large surface area. Many shoes have only a few millimeters of outsole rubber, and some rely mostly on soft foam rather than durable rubber. - **Tires are engineered specifically for abrasion resistance.** Tire compounds, tread patterns, reinforcing belts, and carcasses are optimized to survive tens of thousands of miles while carrying heat, load, and road forces. Shoes balance durability against comfort, flexibility, weight, style, traction, and cost. - **Walking concentrates wear in a few spots.** Most people repeatedly wear down the heel edge and forefoot/toe area. A tire’s tread rotates, spreading use around its entire circumference and width more evenly—assuming proper alignment and inflation. - **Shoes experience more scraping and twisting.** During walking, the heel lands, the foot rolls, and the toe pushes off. Those motions create small amounts of sliding, twisting, and abrasion at the same locations every step. A properly rolling tire has relatively little sliding except during braking, acceleration, turning, or skidding. - **Shoe materials are often softer.** Cushioning foams compress and break down even if the visible outsole still looks acceptable. Tires use highly durable rubber compounds and structural reinforcement, though they also eventually age, crack, and wear out. - **Economics differ.** People generally will not buy or comfortably wear a shoe with tire-like thickness, stiffness, weight, and construction. Tires are expected to be a durable vehicle component; shoes are expected to be light and flexible. So it is not simply that tires carry less load—they often carry enormous loads. They last longer because they have more wear material, distribute wear better, roll rather than repeatedly scuff, and are engineered around long-term durability.
