10:33:20 | INFO | TargetAgent (gpt-5.6-terra) cache: hit=0 miss=667
10:33:20 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Because the damaging part is not only how rough the ground is—it is how the rubber moves against it. A tire rolling straight on a road has very little sliding at its contact patch. It deforms, grips, then rolls onward. Even over gravel or potholes, it has thick reinforced rubber, air cushioning, and a strong internal structure. A shoe sole is thinner and repeatedly: - lands on a small heel area, - scuffs slightly as your foot comes down, - bends sharply at the forefoot, - twists during turns, - drags at the toe during push-off. That concentrates abrasion and flexing in the same few small areas, often on relatively soft rubber or foam. A sidewalk can therefore slowly grind away a shoe sole even though it looks smooth. And tires do wear: many need replacement around 30,000–60,000 miles, sometimes sooner. If shoes had tire-thick, tire-compound outsoles and a similar amount of material, they could last much longer—but they would feel much more like wearing heavy, rigid tire pieces on your feet.
