10:33:36 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Your uncle may be right that they can use some of the same **base rubber families**—such as natural rubber, styrene-butadiene rubber (SBR), or synthetic rubber blends. And some work shoes or specialty soles do use tire-like or recycled-tire rubber. But “literally the same compound” is usually too broad. A *compound* includes much more than the base rubber: fillers like carbon black or silica, oils, curing agents, fibers, hardness, tread depth, and the way the material is molded and bonded. Those choices strongly affect grip, flexibility, heat resistance, and wear. Also, there is no single “tire rubber compound.” A tire’s tread, sidewall, inner liner, and other parts can all use different compounds—and different tire models use different formulas. Shoe manufacturers likewise use many outsole materials: rubber, EVA foam, TPU, polyurethane, blown rubber, carbon rubber, and blends. So the fair version is: - Shoes and tires may share related rubber ingredients. - Some shoe outsoles may be very tire-like. - But ordinary shoe soles and tire treads generally are not identical formulations or constructions. Even if a shoe outsole used the exact same tread rubber as a tire, it would still usually wear differently because it is much thinner, has less material overall, bends at the forefoot, and gets scuffed and twisted in concentrated spots during walking.
