10:33:48 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Rubber products can absolutely be compared. The useful comparison is not “all rubber wears alike,” but “how does this particular rubber part wear under its particular design and use?” Two things can both be rubber and still differ a lot, just as two things can both be steel but one is a spring and another is a bridge cable. A tire and shoe sole can be compared on: - **Rubber hardness and formulation** - **Amount of rubber available to wear away** - **Contact pressure and where it is concentrated** - **Sliding/scuffing versus rolling** - **Flexing and twisting fatigue** - **Heat, load, and exposure conditions** If a shoe sole and tire tread were truly identical in compound, thickness, geometry, and motion against the ground, their wear behavior could be similar. In reality, they differ substantially in thickness, structure, and motion, so their lifetimes differ. That is not saying comparison is impossible; it is saying material name alone does not determine durability.
